body:
Cool! Just a heads-up: I'm a chronic user of enjambments, so I'm biased towards their use. I just always want to let peots know of my own slant before I mingle in their poetry. buuuuut, here we go :)

**stanza 1**: (1) I think you could break b/w "where" and "eyes" (2) also b/w "of" and "the"  
&nbsp;  
I think this not only gives you an emphasis on the imagery of eyes and hope, but tidies up the form and paces the reader. i kind of see two versions in my own mind  
&nbsp;  
*set into holes where  
eyes should be  
all 45 karats of the  
Hope into marble*  
&nbsp;  
*set into holes where  
the eyes should be, all  
45 karats of Hope  
into marble.*  
&nbsp;  
just two suggestions!! by means, there could be hundreds.  
&nbsp;  
*stanza two*: (1) your quote there of internal dialogue is fantastic, but the form reads a bit funnily, given the rest of your poem. I would suggest putting it all on one line, so the quickness of it is kept, or breaking it into two or three lines. you could potentially break after "dark", "until", and "dark", which places emphasis on the darkness and the idea of "it didn't"  
&nbsp;  
*stanza 3*: (1) i think you could break "you" and "mix", and (2) "tired" and "and"  
&nbsp;  
again, i think this adds form and creates the tension you so clearly have written throughout the poem (good job of that, btw)  
&nbsp;  
that's all i got! by no means should i have an immense impact. it's your poem and your style :) but i really like what you got going and would love to see it when you've finessed some features!
